<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The iOS face of the mobile shell — the peer of Shenora.Android, sharing its source verbatim via
    the import below. Today the two differ in nothing but their TFM; the first real divergence is
    expected in the save picker (Android needs SAF, iOS needs UIDocumentPickerViewController), and it
    will land in `Platforms/` on each side rather than behind an #if.

    ⚠ It BUILDS ON WINDOWS and `dev.mjs verify` COMPILES IT. A `net10.0-ios` LIBRARY needs only the
    maui-ios workload and the Microsoft.iOS reference assemblies; a Mac is required to produce and run an
    .app BUNDLE, which is why `_ValidateXcodeVersion` is conditioned on `_CanOutputAppBundle`. Its API
    baseline is gated here too, on Windows, beside Android's — see MetadataSurfaceTests. (`Shenora.slnx`
    carries the same reasoning at length.)

    ⚠ THIS IS WHERE SOMEONE CHECKS WHETHER AN IOS CHANGE IS SAFE TO MAKE FROM WINDOWS, so getting it wrong
    costs more than an ordinary stale comment: it would deny a gate that works. `verify` compiles the iOS
    TFM, which is what catches a doc reference only that TFM rejects (CS1574).
  -->
  <Import Project="..\Shenora.Mobile\Shenora.Mobile.props" />

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net10.0-ios</TargetFrameworks>
    <!-- 15.0 is the .NET 10 iOS workload's floor; MAUI's own template uses the same. -->
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion>15.0</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
    <!--
      Unsafe, for ONE reason: `AudioConverterFillComplexBuffer` pulls its input through a NATIVE CALLBACK,
      so the converter needs an `[UnmanagedCallersOnly]` function pointer and a pinned context. There is no
      safe form of that API, and the safe alternative (`AudioConverterConvertBuffer`) cannot perform a
      complex conversion at all — measured on a device: `'op??'` on the simulator, and success with ZERO
      BYTES on an iPhone.
    -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <!-- Inline on purpose — the baseline coverage gate text-matches it here. See Shenora.Mobile.props. -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>Shenora.iOS</PackageId>
    <Description>Shenora's iOS shell: the MAUI HybridWebView IPC transport (the same envelope the desktop shell speaks, over SendRawMessage/RawMessageReceived), the resource interceptor that answers a page's byte ranges, a UI-thread dispatcher, safe-area insets, and the iOS implementations of the Shenora shell contracts — native file dialogs, an AudioToolbox/VideoToolbox conversion pipeline with device capability probing, an AVPlayer media player, and a Now Playing playback session. The platform owns the loop, so the app drives ShenoraApplication.Start/Stop from its own lifecycle rather than calling Run. Shares its source with Shenora.Android. Also ships the Live Activity devkit: the app writes four SwiftUI view bodies and sets one MSBuild property, and the package supplies the ActivityKit shim, the state mirror and the build step that produces and signs the widget extension.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The Live Activity devkit's two shipped artifacts. Both go under buildTransitive/ so a PackageReference
    picks the targets up automatically and the Swift sits beside it at a path the targets can name.

    ⚠ The SWIFT IS SHIPPED AS SOURCE and that is forced, not a style choice: ActivityKit pairs a running
    activity with a widget by its ActivityAttributes TYPE, and a Swift type's identity includes its MODULE —
    so the attributes must be compiled into the same module as the app's own views. A prebuilt binary could
    never satisfy that. Measured; see .claude/knowledge/mobile-shells.md.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="buildTransitive\Shenora.iOS.targets" Pack="true" PackagePath="buildTransitive\" />
    <!--
      ⚠ The repo path and the PackagePath are deliberately IDENTICAL (`buildTransitive/swift/`). The targets
      find these by `$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)swift/`, so making the source tree mirror the package layout
      means the path the sample exercises is the path a real consumer gets — rather than two paths where only
      one is ever tested. Getting this wrong is how the first Mac build failed.

      🔴 A GLOB, NOT A LIST, AND THAT IS A FIX. This named `ShenoraLiveActivity.swift` alone — correct at
      v0.10.0, when it was the only Swift file, and never extended when `ShenoraLayout.swift` and
      `ShenoraDefaultViews.swift` landed. `ShenoraBuildLiveActivityShim` compiles the layout for EVERY iOS
      app that references this package (unconditional since the 0.9.0 link defect), so the next release
      would have failed every adopter's build at `swiftc: no such file or directory` — while this repo's
      own builds stayed green, because the sample reaches the SOURCE tree and never the nupkg. The 0.9.0
      lesson exactly: only an app-shaped PACKAGE consumer finds this class, so the shape has to make it
      unrepresentable. `LiveActivityPackagingTests` names the file if anyone lists them again.
    -->
    <None Include="buildTransitive\swift\*.swift" Pack="true"
          PackagePath="buildTransitive\swift\" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
